window.SBL_IGCSE_PPQ = {"topic": "Economic Activity and Energy", "img": "/igcse/geography/learn/past-papers-img/", "lessons": {"IGEC01": {"n": 1, "title": "Economic sectors and employment", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l01-economic-sectors-and-employment"}, "IGEC02": {"n": 2, "title": "Factors affecting the location of economic activities", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l02-location-of-economic-activity"}, "IGEC03": {"n": 3, "title": "Changes in sector employment", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l03-changes-in-sector-employment"}, "IGEC04": {"n": 4, "title": "Sector shifts in three countries", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l04-sector-shifts-in-three-countries"}, "IGEC05": {"n": 5, "title": "Informal employment", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l05-informal-employment-bangkok"}, "IGEC06": {"n": 6, "title": "Rising energy demand", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l06-rising-energy-demand"}, "IGEC07": {"n": 7, "title": "Renewable versus non-renewable energy", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l07-renewable-and-non-renewable-energy"}, "IGEC08": {"n": 8, "title": "Case study: fracking in the UK", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l08-case-study-fracking-in-the-uk"}, "IGEC09": {"n": 9, "title": "Sustainable energy", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l09-sustainable-energy"}, "IGEC10": {"n": 10, "title": "Case study: micro-hydro in Peru", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/economic-activity-and-energy/l10-case-study-micro-hydro-in-peru"}}, "parts": [{"key": "P2-2024-1a", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify which employment sector a school teacher belongs to.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "primary"], ["B", "secondary"], ["C", "tertiary"], ["D", "quaternary"]], "answer": "C", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) (1)\n\nC Tertiary (1)\n\nIt cannot be A, B or D as these are not the sectors in which a school teacher would be employed.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1bi", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one cause of employment in the informal sector.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "access to pensions"], ["B", "lack of qualifications"], ["C", "population decline"], ["D", "reliable income"]], "answer": "B", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) (1)\n\nB Lack of qualifications (1)\n\nThe answer cannot be A, C or D as these are not causes of employment in the informal sector.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1bii", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one job that can be found in the informal sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable job that can be found in the (1) informal sector.\n• Street sellers/street food seller (1)\n• Shoe shining (1)\n• Rickshaw drivers (1)\n• Domestic workers (1)\n• Waste picker (1)\n• Textile worker/factory worker (1)\n• Construction workers (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1c", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason why the activities shown are part of the primary sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark) / AO3 (1 mark) (2)\n\nAward 1 mark for basic evidence from the from the figure (AO3) and a further 1 mark for extension (AO2), up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• They are farming (1) which involves the extraction of raw materials (1).\n\n• The workers are harvesting grain (1) which is the extraction of a raw material (1).\n\n• The workers are involved in heavy labour (1) which is typical of the primary sector (1).\n\n• The workers are using a tractor (1) which helps harvest the wheat/extract the raw material (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2024-q1-p50.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2024-1d", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one reason for a decline in employment in the primary sector in many countries.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) (1)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable reason for the decline in the primary sector.\n\n• Mechanisation/increased used of machinery (1)\n• Industrialisation (1)\n• Rural to urban migration (1)\n• Shift to secondary sector industry (1)\n• Increased imports (1)\n• Poor wages (1)\n• Decline in soil fertility/low crop yields (1)\n• Climate change (1)\n\nAccept any other reasonable response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1e", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 4, "text": "For a named developing or emerging country, explain one positive and one negative impact of an economic sector shift.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC04", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\nAward 1 mark for a positive impact and 1 mark for a negative (4) impact and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each. Maximum 2 marks for positive or negative impact.\n\nA range of developing or emerging countries could be chosen as context for this response.\n\nDo not double credit the same impact.\nDo not credit descriptions of increase or decrease in a sector as the impact.\n\nPositive:\n• Increased industrialisation meant the growth of more factories (1) which created more jobs/reduced unemployment.\n• Industrialisation led to greater investment in cities (1) which led to improvements in infrastructure (1).\n• Increased IT services meant some factories closed down (1) which improved air quality in the city (1).\n• Increased TNCs (1) provides more opportunities (1).\n\nNegative:\n• Increased industrialisation meant the growth of more factories (1) which reduced air quality in cities (1).\n• The growth of secondary sector industries led to more rural to urban migration (1) which left many rural areas with fewer workers (1).\n• Mechanisation in factories (1) can lead to reduced jobs available/ increased unemployment (1).\n• Increased TNCs (1) which may have poor working condition (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1f", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one possible reason for a trend in the levels of investment shown.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC09", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks (3)\n\nAward 1 mark (AO1) for basic point about level of investment shown, or specific data from the graph used, and a further 2 marks (AO3) for extension through use of the resource, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• Increased investment in buildings (1) from around $150 to $200bn (1) due to increase in use of renewable energy (1).\n•\n• High level of investment in buildings (1) due to growing populations /rapid urbanisation (1) and the need to reduce carbon emissions (1).\n\n• Overall increase in investment between 2015-2021 (1) as the importance of climate change mitigation is recognised (1) as this can reduce carbon emissions (1).\n\n• Consistently the highest investment in efficiency in buildings (1) may be due potential for retrofitting (1) so can be applied to all buildings in some form (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2024-q1-p51.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2024-1g", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of using hydroelectric power (HEP) to generate electricity.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks) (4)\n\nAward 1 mark for one advantage and one disadvantage, and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\nAdvantage:\n\n• Reduce carbon emissions (1) which can contribute to climate change (1).\n• Energy production can be tailored to demand (1) as water is stored behind a dam (1).\n• Renewable energy source (1) therefore it won’t out (1).\n• Tourism (1) which can support the local economy (1).\n\nDisadvantage\n• Requires construction of large dam (1) which means a large area behind it needs to be flooded/can require flooding of villages/habitats (1).\n• Construction of the dam can interrupt the natural flow of fish (1) which can affect livelihoods downstream (1).\n• Amount of energy produced is not as much as non- renewable energy (1) so need other sources of energy too (1).\n• Requires maintenance (1) which is expensive (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-1h", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(h)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the reasons for differences in the energy mix of these two regions. You must refer to the resource in your answer.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3\n• The energy mix will be affected by the natural resources that are available in different regions of the world.\n• The energy mix will be affected by the environmental conditions that are more suitable for different types of renewable energy sources.\n• For example, areas with large river networks may be more suitable for development of hydro power, and those with more consistent sun conditions may be more suitable for solar power.\n• Levels of development can affect the demand for energy, and therefore potentially a greater demand for energy from a broader range of resources.\n• Access to nuclear power requires high levels of investment which means that it is not always a suitable option for all countries.\n• Investment in renewable energy technologies can be expensive and may not always be the most feasible options for all countries.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows how the EU and Africa have a large proportion of their energy from gas (40%/35%).\n• Figure 1c shows that while they broadly have the same types of energy production there are slight differences in how this is made up.\n• Figure 1c shows Africa makes significantly greater use of hydropower (17% compared to 7%).\n• Figure 1 shows the EU make significantly greater use of oil 15% for Africa compared to EU 31%).\n• Figure 1 shows how the EU make greater use of nuclear power (10% compared to 1% for Africa).\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout.\nA balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2024-q1-p52.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1ai", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one type of employment associated with the primary sector.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "doctor"], ["B", "computer engineer"], ["C", "miner"], ["D", "teacher"]], "answer": "C", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nC miner. (1) (1)\n\nThe answer cannot be A, B or D as these are all tertiary and quaternary sector jobs.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1aii", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one factor that affects the location of primary sector economic activity.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC02", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward one mark for a suitable factor.\n\n• Availability of workforce/labour (1).\n• Proximity to natural resources (1)\n• Availability of land for growing crops (1) .\n• Flat land/relief (1) (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1b", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify a characteristic of informal employment.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "reliable income"], ["B", "consistent income"], ["C", "lack of access to a retail park"], ["D", "lack of unemployment benefits"]], "answer": "D", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nD lack of unemployment benefits (1)\n(1) It cannot be A, B or C as these are not characteristics of informal employment.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1c", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one factor that has affected the location of this business park.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC02", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark) / AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a basic evidence from the from the photograph (AO3) and a further 1 mark for extension through explanation (AO2), up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Open space (1) which means there is potential to expand at a later date.\n\n• Roads (1) to provide good access for the workforce (1).\n\n• Nice/pleasant environment (1) which is designed to act as a benefit for attracting a high quality workforce (1).\n\n(2)\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q1-p54.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1d", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term energy gap.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable definition.\n\n• The difference between a country's demand for energy and its ability to produce that energy from its own resources (1).\n(1)\n\nAccept responses that acknowledge the idea of difference between production and demand.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1e", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 4, "text": "For a named developing or emerging country, explain two ways energy can be managed sustainably.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC10", "ms": "AO2 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for each reason and a further expansion mark or exemplification, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\nMaximum 2 marks where a developed country or no country is provided.\n\nA range of country examples could be chosen.\n\n• [China] Government investment in dams (1) to create more renewable energy through turbines (1)\n• [China] Multiple firms producing electric cars (1) so less fossil fuels used for transport (1).\n• [Morocco] Large investment from government into solar farms [1] to generate electricity from the sun that is readily available in the country.\n• [Morocco] Improving energy efficiency by updating technology (1) to update old solar farms with new technology (1).\n(4)\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1f", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one possible reason for a change in the energy mix shown.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks\n\nAward 1 mark (AO1) for identification of a change and a further 2 marks (AO3) for extension through use of the resource, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• A rise in the use of wind power (1) to reduce carbon emissions (1) and reliance on fossil fuel energy (1).\n\n• A decline in the use of coal (1) to maintain a more sustainable energy mix (1) which is less reliant on fossil fuels (1).\n\n• Development of other renewable energy types such as biomass (1) as country seeks to support a more (3) sustainable energy mix (1) with a more renewable energy (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q1-p55.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1g", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of using renewable energy sources.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for an advantage and a disadvantage, with a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Advantage:\no Renewable sources such as solar do not run out (1) so can help increase energy security (1).\no Wind energy has a potentially infinite supply (1) so it can provide a complement to other energy sources (1).\no Hydroelectric energy using the movement of water can provide an infinite supply of energy (1) but can also create water reserves (dams) (1).\n\n• Disadvantage:\no Some renewable technologies can be expensive (1) so not always accessible to whole populations (1). (4)\no Some renewable resources are not reliable (1) for example where there is no wind, wind energy would not be efficient (1).\no Some renewable energy resources such as tidal can only be located in certain places (1) so not always an option for national networks (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-1h", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(h)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse reasons for changes in the employment sectors shown. Refer to the resource in your answer.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3\n• Developed based around the changes that took place in the UK the Clark-Fisher model provides an overview of employment changes that a country could typically go through as it moved from being a pre-industrial country to a post-industrial country.\n• There are three distinct phases in the model:\no Pre-industrial phase where the primary sector dominates the economy and tends to account for over two thirds of employment. Agriculture tends to be the dominant activity.\no Industrial phase where the secondary and tertiary sectors increase in productivity. The secondary sector tends to peak in this phase but does not tend to account for mor than two thirds of employment.\no Post-industrial phase which involves a fall in primary and secondary sectors, a rise in tertiary sector employment and the emergence of the quaternary sector.\n• Reasons for the changes:\no Mechanisation of agriculture reduces requirement for labourers which can then move to other sectors (with the potential for higher wages).\no A rise in secondary sector businesses tends to dominate in cities, which can lead to increased rural-urban migration which not only fuels the secondary sector but the growth of the tertiary sector too.\no Global shift of manufacturing in some developed countries can lead to a reduced secondary sector and the rise of tertiary and quaternary.\no Advances in technology allows for reduced labour required in the secondary sector, but also the spread of service based employment in the tertiary and quaternary sectors. o\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows the Clark-Fisher model which shows how employment structures typically change over time within a country.\n• On Figure 1c it can be seen that in pre-industrial countries there is a dominance of the primary sector.\n• On Figure 1c it can be seen that in industrial countries there is a rise initially in the secondary sector, followed by the tertiary sector.\n• Figure 1c shows how post-industrial countries have high tertiary sector employment, with rising quaternary sector employment and falling primary and secondary employment.\n\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q1-p56.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-1ai", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one advantage of using geothermal energy as a source of energy.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "It uses the power of the sun."], ["B", "It uses the power of the wind."], ["C", "It is a type of non-renewable energy."], ["D", "It is a type of renewable energy."]], "answer": "D", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nD It is a source of renewable energy (1)\n\nThe answer cannot be A (solar power), B (wind power), or C (1) (incorrect).", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1aii", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term renewable energy.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\n• a source of energy that cannot be exhausted (1)\n• energy that can be used again and again (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1b", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one way to make transport more sustainable.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "increasing the use of powerful petrol engines."], ["B", "increasing air travel routes."], ["C", "increasing the use of electric battery technology."], ["D", "increasing the size of motorways."]], "answer": "C", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC09", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nC Increasing the use of electric battery technology (1)\n\nThe answer cannot be A, B or D as these all require increased resource consumption and therefore are not sustainable. (1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1ci", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(i)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason why the activity shown might be considered informal employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark) / AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for basic evidence from the figure (AO3) and a further 1 mark for extension through explanation (AO2), up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Selling food in the street (1) rather than in a designated market (1).\n\n• Stall does not look permanent (1) so it is probably not a designated shop space (1).\n• There are no signs or labels for the goods being sold (1) which suggests there is no record of sales (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q1-p46.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-1cii", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one advantage of informal employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable advantage.\n\n• Often requires less skills/qualifications (1).\n• Unlikely to pay tax (1).\n• Requires little investment to set up informal business (1).\n• Reducing unemployment (1).\n\nAccept any other reasonable response. (1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1d", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two factors that affect global energy demand.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a factor and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Increasing population (1) means that there is a greater demand for energy, e.g. petrol for cars (1).\n\n• Economic development can lead to more industry (1) which requires greater use of resources (1).\n\n• Awareness of climate change (1) can lead to an increase in demand for renewable energy sources (1).\n\n• Increased use of technology (1) leads to greater consumption of energy (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\n(4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1e", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Explain the relationship shown between population growth and food supply.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks\n\nAward 1 mark (AO1) for a basic idea and a further 2 marks (AO3) for extension through use of the resource, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• Malthus’s theory states that population would grow exponentially (1) whereas food production would grow more slowly (linear growth) (1). This would lead to population outstripping food growth (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(3)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q1-p47.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-1f", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two factors that affect the location of quaternary sector employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC02", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Good transport connections (1) as companies need good access to road and rail connections to allow a skilled workforce to reach them.\n\n• Reliable internet access (1) as many companies rely on this to operate their business (1).\n\n• Proximity to a university (1) as they can provide a skilled workforce suitable for the sector (1).\n\n• Large area of land available (1) to allow room for growth (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-1g", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the reasons for the changes in the percentage of people employed in the secondary sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3\n• The relative size of secondary sector employment is a good indicator of economic development.\n• As a country becomes more developed, typically there will be a shift from primary sector employment to more secondary sector employment.\n• Countries which previously have been dominated by primary sector employment develop the capacity to process raw materials so they can gain more money from their sale and exports, than they would through the sale of raw materials.\n• In developed countries, as their economy advances there can also be a shift from secondary employment to more tertiary and quaternary employment.\n• In many developed countries there has been a decline in secondary sector employment as many of these jobs are now outsourced to countries with lower costs.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows that different countries have different trends for employment in the secondary sector.\n• Figure 1c shows that for developed countries such as the UK and the US, there has been a steady decline in the percentage employed in the secondary sector.\n• Figure 1c shows that Cambodia has experienced a steady rise in secondary sector employment.\n• Figure 1c shows how Ghana has only more recently experienced a rise in secondary sector employment compared to other countries shown, from around 2013.\n• Figure 1c shows how China has experienced different rates of growth in the percentage of the secondary sector, with more rapid rise from around 2001-2012.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q1-p48.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1ai", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one disadvantage of using natural gas as a source of energy.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "There is an infinite supply."], ["B", "It is easy to export."], ["C", "It is a renewable energy source."], ["D", "Burning it releases CO2."]], "answer": "D", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nD Burning it releases CO2 (1)\n\nThe answer cannot be A (refers to oil), B (an advantage), C (incorrect). (1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1aii", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term non-renewable energy.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\n• Energy produced from resources that cannot be replaced once they are used (1).\n• Energy produced from fossil fuels (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1b", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one way to make a factory more sustainable.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "Increase the factory size."], ["B", "Increase the number of vehicles used to transport goods."], ["C", "Use more packaging for goods produced."], ["D", "Use more renewable energy."]], "answer": "D", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC09", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nD Use more renewable energy (1)\n(1) It cannot be A, B or C as these would make a factory less sustainable.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1ci", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(i)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason why this might be considered primary sector employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark) / AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for basic evidence from the from the photograph (AO3) and a further 1 mark for extension through explanation (AO2), up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• People are involved in farming which is in the primary sector (1) they are ploughing prior to transplanting (1).\n• It involves farming (1) and is labour intensive (1).\n\n• Primary sector involves extraction of raw materials (1) here they are preparing the land to grow crops (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\n(2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q1-p46.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1cii", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Name one raw material produced in the primary sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable raw material.\n\n• Coal/Oil/Gas (1)\n• Fish (1)\n• Crops (1)\n• Timber/Wood (1) (1)\n\nAccept other suitable raw materials.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1d", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two factors that affect global energy production.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Availability of natural resources (1) if new sources of oil are found then production will continue to go up (1).\n\n• If demand shifts to more renewable energy sources (1) then efforts will be made to produce more, leading to less demand for non-renewable sources (1).\n\n• Conflict in areas that produce energy resources (1) can lead to disrupted production and lower levels of production overall (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1e", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Explain the relationship shown between population growth and food supply.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks\n\nAward 1 mark (AO1) for a basic idea and a further 2 marks (AO3) for extension through use of the resource, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• Boserup believed that as the population grew people would always find ways for food production to be higher than needed, (1) as people would invest in new ways to increase food supply (1) e.g. chemical fertilisers to improve yield (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (3)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q1-p47.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1f", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two factors that affect the location of secondary sector industries.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC02", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for each factor and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Good transport facilities (1) to ensure goods produced can reach the market efficiently (1).\n\n• Access to reliable supplies of electricity (1) to ensure production of goods is not disrupted (1).\n\n• Access to labour force (1) to ensure that there are sufficient workers to enable production (1).\n\n• Proximity to raw material (1) as they are heavy to (4) transport (1).\n\n• Availability of land (1) as they may need room to expand (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022R-1g", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the reasons for the differences in the percentage of people employed in the tertiary sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3\n• The employment structure of a country is often related to the development stage of a country.\n• The change in employment structure according to the Clark Fisher model means countries move to higher proportions of tertiary sector employment in the post-industrial phase, with increasing amounts of tertiary sector employment in the industrial phase while primary and secondary sectors experience relative decline.\n• Tertiary sector employment involves providing services, this can include commercial (e.g. retail), professional (e.g. legal services), social (e.g. education/healthcare), entertainment (e.g. cinemas) or personal (e.g. hairdressers).\n• Developing or emerging countries are likely to be in the industrial phase where there is a rise in the tertiary industry, with some moving to the post industrial phase.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows the percentage of employment in the tertiary sector for a range of developed, developing and emerging countries.\n• On Figure 1c it can be seen that developed countries such as Japan have had a high proportion of tertiary sector employment since 1991 with further increases to over 70% by 2019.\n• Figure 1c shows how some countries such as Cambodia have had a larger increase in the proportion of employment in the tertiary sector since 1991, an increase of around 25 percentage points.\n• Figure 1c shows that while some countries have experienced a steady increase in tertiary sector employment, others have had more variable rates of change. For example, Egypt which rose until around 2003, had a small decline to 2008 and then slowly increasing again to 2019.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q1-p48.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-1ai", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term energy security.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable definition.\n\n• The abililty to provide sufficient, affordable and consistent energy supplies to match demand (1).\n• The ability of a country to secure sufficient, affordable and consistent energy supplies (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response which refers to just one aspect of energy security.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1aii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Identify the two forms of non-renewable energy.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (2 marks)\n\nA - Coal (1)\nD - Oil (1)\n\nB, C, E, and F are incorrect as they are renewable sources of energy.\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1b", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one way this house has been adapted to manage energy consumption.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC09", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark) / A03 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for identification of adaptation method evident from the photograph (AO3), and a further 1 mark for extension through explanation (AO2), up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Solar panels (1) have been installed to reduce reliance on energy from the national grid (1).\n\n• Large windows (1) to maximise temperature gains from sunlight during the day (1).\n\n• Double glazed windows / curtains or blinds (1) to reduce loss of heat through windows (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. Evidence must be plausibly inferred from the photograph, e.g. windows likely to be double-glazed.\n(2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q1-p50.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-1ci", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one characteristic of the tertiary economic sector.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following:\n\n• Usually involves provision of services. (1)\n• Can involve selling goods and products from primary and secondary industries. (1)\n• Dominant in developed countries. (1)\n• Workers usually need to be highly skilled /qualified. (1)\n• White-collar workers. (1)\n\nDo not accept a job that would usually be found in the tertiary sector unless it linked to a characteristic e.g. teacher working in a school, providing education.\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1cii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)(ii)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two factors that affect the location of tertiary sector activities.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC02", "ms": "AO2 (2 + 2 marks)\nAward 1 mark for a suitable factor that affects the location of tertiary industries, and a further mark for a development of this point, up to maximum of 2 marks per explanation.\n• Transport links (1) to ensure workers can access the site. (1)\n• Fast communications provision (1) as usually telecommunications are an important component of operations. (1)\n• Access to skilled labour (1) to ensure suitable workers are available. (1)\n• Reliable power supply (1) as often this is needed for technology involved. (1)\n• Access to market (1) to ensure it remains profitable. (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1di", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Study Figure 1b below. It shows employment structures for selected countries in 1991 and 2018. Label the box with an X to show which pie chart displays the largest percentage for secondary sector employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "draw", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO3 (1 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for:\n\nBox pointing to India in 2018.\n\n(1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q1-fig1b.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-1dii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify which one of the following is correct.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "\u0007Brazil’s employment in the tertiary/quaternary sectors has increased by 12 percentage points."], ["B", "\u0007India’s employment in tertiary/quaternary sectors has increased by 12 percentage points."], ["C", "\u0007The UK’s employment in the primary sector has decreased by 12 percentage points."], ["D", "\u0007India’s employment in the primary sector has decreased by 12 percentage points."]], "answer": "B", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nB - India’s employment in tertiary/quaternary sectors has increased by 12 percentage points (1).\n\nAll the other options are incorrect.\n\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1diii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)(iii)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one reason for decline in primary sector employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO2 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable reason.\n\n• Increased mechanization. (1)\n• Industrialisation. (1)\n• Rural to urban migration. (1)\n• Exhaustion of raw materials. (1)\n• Increased level of education for local population. (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1e", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 4, "text": "For a named megacity explain two advantages of informal employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO2 (4 marks)\nAward 1 mark for identification of an advantage of informal employment and an additional 1 mark for development through further explanation or exemplification. Maximum of 2 marks when no named megacity.\n\nResponses will vary depending on megacity chosen.\n\n• [Mumbai]\no Working as a rickshaw driver provides opportunity for income (1) without having to pay taxes (1).\no Selling street food in Dharavi but without needing a fixed building to sell produce (1) which means they don’t need to pay rent (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-1f", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the possible reasons why energy consumption has grown.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\n\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include the following.\n\nAO3\n• As an emerging country China’s energy use has been increasing at a rapid rate to fuel industrialisation and expansion of the economy. As countrys develop their need for energy grows.\n• China has also experienced rapid growth in population over the last few decades (981 million in 1980 to 1.37 billion in 2014). Such growth in population will cause an increase in energy demand.\n• As the country industrialises it has greater need for energy for industry and technology from coal power plants to high tech industries.\n• As the countries income rises, as indicated by GDP capita, populations often desire more consumer goods, many of which require energy in their manufacturing.\n• Increased wealth is often associated with increased car ownership for example which creates more demand for energy which given the size of China’s population gives good justfication for the rate of growth shown in energy use.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows an increase in energy use and GDP per capita in China across several decades.\n• The two pieces of data follow a similar trend of slow growth in the 1980s and 1990s.\n• Energy use begins to rapidly increase in 2000, followed by GDP in 2005.\n• Figure 1c shows how the rate of growth of GDP has been rapid for China since 2005.\n• The rate of growth for energy use has slowed down slightly 2010-2014.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q1-p51.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019-1a", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify what is meant by an economic sector.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "the chain of production in manufacturing"], ["B", "an economic shift in employment"], ["C", "a classification system for types of employment"], ["D", "a classification of employment structures"]], "answer": "C", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nC a classification system for types of employment (1)\n\nC is the correct answer as the other options do not related to economic sectors.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1b", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify the economic sector that includes agriculture as an activity.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "quaternary"], ["B", "tertiary"], ["C", "secondary"], ["D", "primary"]], "answer": "D", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nD primary\n(1)\nD is the correct answer since the other options exclude agriculture as part of their activity mix.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1c", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term raw material.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• the basic material from which a product is made (1).\n• natural resources which are unprocessed (1).\n• an unrefined material straight from source / in its natural state (1).\n• natural resources for use / processing by the secondary sector (1).\n• naturally occurring (resource) used to make another product (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, including forms of energy e.g. crude oil, coal etc.\n\nResponse must include and idea of it being unprocessed, raw, unfinished or going to be used by the secondary sector for credit.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1d", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one piece of evidence that shows there is informal employment in this photograph.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC05", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark) / AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark (AO3) for evidence from the image and a further mark (AO2) for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks:\n\n• There are street sellers selling fruit next to a car (1) as they do not have a fixed building to sell produce from (1)\n\n• In the foreground there is a man on a bike carrying goods (1) because the economy is still reliant on the cash economy (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(2)\nNote evidence must be directly from photograph, not assumed.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019-q1-p50.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019-1e", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one example of a renewable energy resource.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• Wind / wind turbines / wind power (1)\n\n• Solar / solar PV / solar power / solar energy (1)\n\n• Biomass / wood (1)\n\n• Geothermal (1)\n(1)\n• Hydro / pumped storage / HEP (1)\n\n• Tidal (1)\n\nAccept nuclear.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1f", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two reasons why the production of energy varies between countries.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO2 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Availability of energy resources (1) places which have a lot, e.g. oil, often consume in high amounts (1).\n\n• Price of energy (1) will impact on supply and demand within a region or country (1).\n\n• Some places countries have larger populations (1) therefore will demand more energy (1).\n\n• In some hot countries, they require more cooling (1) so more energy is needed to operate fridges and air-conditioning (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, e.g. related to level of development.\n(4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1g", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason for the projected increase in energy demand in Asia.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks\n\nAward 1 mark (AO1) for a basic reason and a further 2 marks (AO3) for extension through explanation or description, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• The big increase between 2020 and 2040 350-475 BTU (1) due an increase in global population (1) which means overall there will be a substantial increase in consumption (1).\n\n• The big increase between 2020 and 2040 350-475 BTU (1) due an increase in consumption per head (1) a people become wealthier they generally have more appliances and devices which use more energy (1).\n\n• Asia shows the biggest overall increase approx. 200 t0 300 BTU over 2020-2040 (1) this may be caused by the continued growth in manufacturing (1) which uses large amounts of energy in the process (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (3)\nNote: data not required as part of response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019-q1-p51.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019-1h", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(h)", "marks": 4, "text": "For a named developing or emerging country, explain two ways the increasing demand for energy has created problems.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO2 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for initial explanation and an additional 1 mark for development through further explanation or exemplification.\nMaximum of 2 marks when no named developing country is used in context, or a developed country is used instead.\n\nA range of countries and examples could be chosen, based on either social, economic or environmental problems.\n\n• In China the government have promoted the use of coal to generate electricity (1) this is because there is an energy gap and coal is plentiful within China (1).\n\n• People in China have become wealthier and now many people have cars which need fossil fuel as a source of energy (1). The increased use of cars is contributing to a range of local as well as national pollution issues alongside worries about enhanced greenhouse effect (1).\n\n• In China the government has recently encouraged new heavy manufacturing plants to relocation in some cities that are rapidly growing (1) therefore increasing the demand for electricity to run the machinery which is causing local air pollution issues (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nNote: Accept a city, e.g. Hong Kong can be accepted as China. (4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-1i", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 1, "id": "1(i)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the reasons for changes in primary and quaternary employment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3\n• The primary sector is working with natural resources which includes farming, fishing, forestry mining and quarrying.\n• The quaternary sector is involved in information and communications as well as R&D and development. Universities are an important part of this sector.\n• The relative importance of primary and quaternary sectors (together with secondary and tertiary) is a good indicator of the level of economic development.\n• In general terms a LHD / developing economy relies heavily on the primary sector, whereas a county with VHHD has a recently, but rapidly growing quaternary sector.\n• Sectoral shifts are wedded to the development process (pathway) and it is a result of several other factors that come into play, including rural- urban migration, increasing wealth as well as wider considerations such as globalisation and developments in ICT.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows the decrease in primary from the pre-industrial, to industrial to post-industrial stages.\n• On Figure 1c, it can be seen that the most dramatic (steepest) drop in employment occurs for the primary sector during the industrial phase.\n• Figure 1c shows that absolute decline in numbers employed in primary sector, post-industrial, are relatively low.\n• Figure 1c shows that the in the post-industrial phase there is a growth of quaternary, starting from a 0 base.\n• Overall the percentage employed in quaternary remains low in the post- industrial, peaking at about 5%\n\nNote only credit information and discussions around primary and quaternary employment.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout.\nA balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019-q1-p52.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1a", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "text": "The Clark Fisher model is used to describe the process of industrialisation. Identify the stage which is characterised by agriculture being the most important sector.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "pre-industrial phase"], ["B", "industrial phase"], ["C", "development pathway phase"], ["D", "post-industrial phase"]], "answer": "A", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nA pre-industrial (1)\n\nA is the correct answer as the other options do not relate to the correct stage with agriculture.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1b", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Define the term employment structure.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• How the workforce is divided up between the three main employment sectors - primary, secondary and tertiary. (1).\n• The proportions / percentage of people in the primary, secondary and tertiary workforce. (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1c", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(c)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify the economic sector that includes manufacturing as an activity.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "quaternary"], ["B", "tertiary"], ["C", "secondary"], ["D", "primary"]], "answer": "C", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nC Secondary\n(1)\nC is the correct answer as the other options do not include manufacturing.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1d", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(d)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 1a in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason that has influenced the proportions of GDP shown in Turkey.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC01", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark) / AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark (AO3) for a reason from the resource and a further mark (AO1) for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks:\n\n• Tertiary and quaternary is the largest sector (1) because they have a well-educated labour force (1).\n\n• 27% of manufacturing (secondary) is the highest (1) because Turkey is developing its manufacturing economy as part of its industrialisation (1).\n• Primary, e.g. agriculture is nearly ¼ of the GDP (1) because the economy is still reliant on the primary sector (1). (2)\n\n• Globalisation has allowed the country to received more foreign investment (1) causing the growth of the tertiary and quaternary sectors (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, e.g. growth in air travel, tourism, containerisation, high skilled workforce, rural urban migration etc.\n\nNote: Just stating the data, with no reason can get 1 mark.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019R-q1-p46.jpg", "figs": ["1a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1e", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(e)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one example of a non-renewable energy resource.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC07", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• Coal (1)\n\n• Oil / Tar sands (1)\n\n• Natural gas (1)\n\n• Shale gas (1)\n(1)\n\nAccept nuclear.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1f", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(f)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two reasons why energy demand varies globally.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO2 (2+2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\n• Availability of energy resources (1) places which have a lot, e.g. oil, often consume in high amounts as its cheaper (1).\n\n• Price of energy within a country (1) will impact on supply and demand within a region or country (1).\n• Some places countries have larger populations (1) therefore will demand more energy (1).\n\n• In some hot countries, they require more cooling to keep people comfortable (1) so more energy is needed to operate fridges and air-conditioning (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, e.g. factors such as (4) population density.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1g", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(g)", "marks": 3, "text": "Study Figure 1b in the Resource Booklet. Suggest one reason for the shift in manufacturing production shown on Figure 1b.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC03", "ms": "AO1 (1 mark) / (AO3) 2 marks\n\nAward 1 mark (AO3) for an idea from the resource and a further 2 marks (AO2) for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\n• Employment costs may be cheaper overseas (1) due to the workforce being based in an emerging economy country (1) which means the manufacturer saves overall costs (1).\n\n• Advances in technology have allowed manufacturing to relocate overseas (1) which has stimulated the growth of new jobs in this industry (1) but still maintained the quality of the finished product (1).\n\n• Places such as China offer car manufacturers new markets (1) so they can sell more vehicles when manufactured locally (1) and ultimately make more profit for shareholders (1).\n\n• Fords preference for China rather than Mexico is due to costs of production (1). China has a large labour supply and can offer competitive labour rates (1) so Ford is making a decision to move (3) to the lowest cost country to maximise returns (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019R-q1-p47.jpg", "figs": ["1b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1h", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(h)", "marks": 4, "text": "For a named developing or emerging country, explain two ways energy resources have been managed in a sustainable way.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC10", "ms": "AO2 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for initial explanation and an additional 1 mark for development through further explanation or exemplification.\nMaximum of 2 marks (1 per way) when no named developing / emerging country is used in context, or a developed country is used instead.\n\nA range of countries and examples could be chosen, based on either increasing production, switching to using “greener” approaches, or reducing demand, e.g. with policies and incentives.\n\n• In China the government have promoted the use of solar panels to generate electricity (1) this is because they are environmentally low carbon and cheaper than non- renewable energy (1).\n\n• China have become much more energy self-sufficient through the use of government polices to encourage HEP (1). This is a cleaner technology which doesn’t reply on harmful fossil fuel combustion (1).\n\n• In Bejing (China) the government has recently encouraged all new buildings to have better insulation for hot summer temperatures (1) therefore reducing electricity demand for electric A/C in the summer (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(4)\nNote: Accept a city, e.g. Mumbai can be accepted as India.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-1i", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 1, "id": "1(i)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 1c in the Resource Booklet. Analyse the reasons for the different population and resource relationships shown.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGEC06", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\n\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\nAO3\n• Malthus thought that if the human population continued to grow, food production would not be able to keep up with demand and there would not be enough food to go around. The result, he warned, would be a terrible famine that would kill many people.\n• Boserup suggested that food production can, and will, increase to match the needs of the population - Boserup described this change as ‘agricultural intensification’.\n• Boserup used her knowledge of farming in the developing world, where populations were growing quickly, Boserup argued that the threat of starvation and the challenge of feeding more mouths motivates people to improve their farming methods and invent new technologies in order to produce more food.\n• In contrast Malthus was arguing that the human population was at risk of outgrowing its carrying capacity (the number of individuals that can be supported by a specific habitat). At the time when he was writing the Industrial Revolution had not yet arrived, and without developments such as pesticides and fertilisers the amount of food that could be produced per acre of land was much smaller than it is today.\n\nAO4\n• Figure 1c shows the differences between two population theories, with both increasing demand for food and a growing population.\n• On Figure 1c, it can be seen that the models are very different to each other, with different assumptions about futures.\n• Figure 1c shows that Malthus assumes a linear or steady relationship between population and food resources, but where the population line (red) intersects the model (green line) then there is a collapse or catastrophe.\n• Figure 1c shows that the Boserup theory indicates that the food resource can keep pace with the increase in population, and the red line never intersects the blue line of the model.\n• Overall the population increases at an exponential rate.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 1–3 • Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements that are supported by limited evidence. (AO3)\n• Uses some geographical skills to obtain information with limited relevance and accuracy, which supports few aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports some aspects of the argument. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 • Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout.\nA balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3)\n• Uses geographical skills to obtain accurate information that supports all aspects of the argument. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019R-q1-p48.jpg", "figs": ["1c"]}]}]};
